                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Pros:\n- Nice music \n- Attaching characters, including the MC\n- Pretty good art\n- The collecting of money / items isn't repetitive: you don't have to grind much to make the most out of the game\n- Just enough replay value\n- The plot is not bad at all\n\nCons:\n- Relatively expensive for just one route. But there are much worse visual novel on Steam for the same price.\n- Epilogue and side story are disappointing (much too short and almost random whereas there is all this moe power going to waste). And don't point the rating; the devs know how they built their game and what they did. \nMost visual novels that include adult content doesn't have a plot, and their characters are bland, boring. It's rare to have an otome that tackle realist life problems and succeed at making characters who grow along the plot. When you have that kind of material, it feels weird not to use it at all. \nZeiva, you have to reconsider the way to write your epilogues.\n\n\nOkay, enough whining about otome rated R for no reason.\n(...But still; visual novels for boys and men doesn't often have this problem. If there is a reason for that, let me tell you the difference gape is much too wide. As a result, this trend looks just silly.) \n\nOverall, I've been waiting for Sheep's path for years, and I almost forgot this game existed until now. \n...And the result was worth waiting for. I wasn't disappointed, even though there could have been more content-wise.\n\nI noticed that, on the official website, in the character list they feature Lion, Elephant and Snail as if they were playable, but nothing indicate that their route are in prduction shedule at all. \n\nOwl, Pig, and Monkey are officially non playable ( one is too old, one is too young, and the last is only a very cute and smart Monkey. This only gives relief to the whole ensemble. ... And (of course) makes my want to have friendship routes with them even more. lol\n\nI hope that Zeiva will produce and publish Lion, Elephant, and Snail one day. But since they already published Anion Party, which is the series conclusion, this looks unseeming.\n\nAnyway, Anicon has been one among very rare otome games that didn't bore me dry with pointless chit chat, silly teenage drama, artwork that hurts the eyes, or grinding. \n \nThere is few evaluations, but bad visibility doesn't mean that the game is subpar: sometimes it's even the contradictory. Anicon is one of the most polished short visual novels out there to this day.",
